1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,714
Laddoo Lab now sells five things.

2
00:00:02,714 --> 00:00:05,560
But Zoya has money for only one big push.

3
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:07,534
Which product should get it?

4
00:00:07,534 --> 00:00:09,336
And should anything be dropped?

5
00:00:09,886 --> 00:00:14,983
Managing a family of products like this
is called product portfolio management.

6
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:17,045
Here is the range.

7
00:00:17,045 --> 00:00:20,377
The original ragi six-pack,
and the new cocoa-ragi.

8
00:00:20,727 --> 00:00:23,041
Coconut-jowar, made for gym-goers.

9
00:00:23,041 --> 00:00:26,190
And a festive gift box,
planned for Diwali.

10
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:29,706
There are also loose big
laddoos at the college stall.

11
00:00:29,706 --> 00:00:31,454
A few still sell each week.

12
00:00:32,104 --> 00:00:34,335
So what is a product portfolio?

13
00:00:34,635 --> 00:00:37,812
It is the full set of
products a company sells.

14
00:00:37,812 --> 00:00:40,006
Kotler calls it the product mix.

15
00:00:40,356 --> 00:00:43,059
Its width is the number of product lines.

16
00:00:43,059 --> 00:00:46,889
Laddoo Lab has three: six-packs,
gifts, and loose laddoos.

17
00:00:47,239 --> 00:00:49,580
Its length is all the products: five.

18
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,384
Its depth is the flavours
in one line: three.

19
00:00:52,734 --> 00:00:56,420
Portfolio management means
deciding where the money goes.

20
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:58,211
Build, keep, or drop.

21
00:00:58,861 --> 00:01:03,891
In 1970, Bruce Henderson of the Boston
Consulting Group shared a simple

22
00:01:03,891 --> 00:01:06,212
tool: the growth-share matrix.

23
00:01:06,562 --> 00:01:08,126
It asks two questions.

24
00:01:08,126 --> 00:01:10,909
Up the side: is the market growing fast?

25
00:01:11,259 --> 00:01:14,568
Along the bottom: is our share
big, compared with rivals?

26
00:01:14,918 --> 00:01:16,557
That makes four boxes.

27
00:01:16,557 --> 00:01:18,990
Let's place each Laddoo Lab product.

28
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:20,240
Cocoa-ragi.

29
00:01:20,209 --> 00:01:23,684
Its market grows fast,
and Laddoo Lab leads it.

30
00:01:23,684 --> 00:01:24,584
A star.

31
00:01:24,806 --> 00:01:26,306
The original six-pack.

32
00:01:26,306 --> 00:01:30,258
Its market has stopped growing,
but Laddoo Lab still leads.

33
00:01:30,258 --> 00:01:31,251
A cash cow.

34
00:01:31,601 --> 00:01:33,883
Coconut-jowar and the gift box.

35
00:01:33,883 --> 00:01:36,911
Growing markets, but few buyers so far.

36
00:01:36,911 --> 00:01:38,158
Question marks.

37
00:01:38,508 --> 00:01:40,313
And the loose big laddoos.

38
00:01:40,313 --> 00:01:42,674
A slow market, and a small share.

39
00:01:42,674 --> 00:01:43,574
A dog.

40
00:01:43,677 --> 00:01:45,058
Now the money moves.

41
00:01:45,058 --> 00:01:47,495
The cash cow earns more than it needs.

42
00:01:47,845 --> 00:01:50,888
That cash feeds the star,
and one question mark.

43
00:01:50,888 --> 00:01:53,597
Zoya picks the gift box, for the festival.

44
00:01:53,947 --> 00:01:56,261
Coconut-jowar stays small for now.

45
00:01:56,261 --> 00:01:57,344
And the dog?

46
00:01:57,344 --> 00:01:59,185
The matrix says drop it.

47
00:01:59,835 --> 00:02:01,030
Milk the cows.

48
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:02,279
Feed the stars.

49
00:02:02,279 --> 00:02:04,226
Choose your question marks.

50
00:02:04,926 --> 00:02:06,980
The matrix points to four moves.

51
00:02:07,330 --> 00:02:08,230
Build.

52
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:11,238
Spend to grow a star, or
a promising question mark.

53
00:02:11,538 --> 00:02:12,438
Hold.

54
00:02:12,082 --> 00:02:14,134
Keep a cash cow's share steady.

55
00:02:14,434 --> 00:02:15,334
Harvest.

56
00:02:15,119 --> 00:02:18,243
Take the cash out, and
spend less on promotion.

57
00:02:18,543 --> 00:02:19,443
Divest.

58
00:02:19,164 --> 00:02:22,315
Sell or drop a weak
product, and free the money.

59
00:02:22,965 --> 00:02:24,932
Where do we see this at work today?

60
00:02:25,282 --> 00:02:26,182
Stars.

61
00:02:25,928 --> 00:02:29,728
A phone maker pours money into
its fastest-selling model.

62
00:02:30,078 --> 00:02:30,978
Cash cows.

63
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:33,920
A bank's savings accounts
pay for its new app.

64
00:02:34,270 --> 00:02:35,403
Question marks.

65
00:02:35,403 --> 00:02:39,305
A food delivery app tries
grocery delivery in a few cities.

66
00:02:39,655 --> 00:02:40,555
Dogs.

67
00:02:40,266 --> 00:02:43,582
An airline drops a route
that few people fly.

68
00:02:44,232 --> 00:02:46,474
But is the matrix always right?

69
00:02:46,474 --> 00:02:47,429
Not always.

70
00:02:47,779 --> 00:02:48,785
The good side.

71
00:02:48,785 --> 00:02:52,454
It shows the whole range at
once, and where cash should flow.

72
00:02:52,804 --> 00:02:53,854
The hard side.

73
00:02:53,854 --> 00:02:58,512
It looks at only two things, and the
line between high and low is a guess.

74
00:02:58,862 --> 00:03:01,445
Dev wants to drop the loose laddoos.

75
00:03:01,445 --> 00:03:02,627
Arjun says no.

76
00:03:02,627 --> 00:03:05,681
Students try them first,
then buy six-packs.

77
00:03:06,031 --> 00:03:09,008
So Zoya keeps a few,
as a cheap first taste.

78
00:03:09,358 --> 00:03:14,720
In 1992, Slater and Zwirlein found
that firms investing by such a model

79
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:17,712
earned weaker returns for shareholders.

80
00:03:18,062 --> 00:03:18,972
The lesson?

81
00:03:18,972 --> 00:03:23,243
Use the matrix to ask good questions,
not to make every decision.

82
00:03:23,893 --> 00:03:24,793
Quick check.

83
00:03:24,787 --> 00:03:28,620
A product leads its market, but
the market has stopped growing.

84
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:29,523
What is it?

85
00:03:31,423 --> 00:03:32,376
A cash cow!

86
00:03:32,376 --> 00:03:35,371
High share, low growth,
and plenty of cash.

87
00:03:35,971 --> 00:03:36,984
So, remember.

88
00:03:37,284 --> 00:03:41,516
A portfolio is all your products,
with a width, a length and a depth.

89
00:03:41,866 --> 00:03:46,483
The growth-share matrix sorts them
into stars, cash cows, question marks

90
00:03:46,483 --> 00:03:47,383
and dogs.

91
00:03:47,682 --> 00:03:51,817
Then cash from the cows builds the
stars and the best question marks.

92
00:03:52,417 --> 00:03:57,108
Next time, Laddoo Lab gets a real
identity: branding fundamentals.
